{"title": "CSCI-531: Introduction to Security Measurement", "learningObjectives": [
        "Define the four practice groups of the Secure Software Development Framework and explain their roles in secure development.",
        "Explain how cryptographic standards define approved algorithms and parameters, and how modes of operation process data.",
        "Apply security reasoning to identify assets, trust boundaries, threats, controls, and residual risk in a software system."
      ], "beats": [
        {
          "title": "Course Foundations and Prerequisites",
          "claim": "CSCI-531 introduces algorithmic foundations and modern methods for security evaluation and tools design, requiring prerequisites like CSCI-351 or equivalent courses.",
          "example": "Students must verify they have completed CSCI-351 or the combined 4003-420 and 4003-440 sequence before enrolling.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a prerequisite flowchart showing CSCI-351 or the 4003-420/4003-440 path leading to CSCI-531 enrollment.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-531"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Secure Software Development Framework Overview",
          "claim": "The Secure Software Development Framework groups practices into four practice groups: Prepare the Organization, Protect the Software, Produce Well-Secured Software, and Respond to ",
          "example": "Prepare the Organization establishes people, processes, and technology for secure development, while Protect the Software prevents unauthorized access to code.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a four-quadrant diagram labeling each practice group with its core function.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ssdf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Prepare the Organization Practice",
          "claim": "Prepare the Organization practice establishes people, processes, and technology for secure development, forming the foundation for all subsequent security activities.",
          "example": "A team creates a secure development policy and assigns security roles before writing code begins.",
          "visualDirection": "Illustrate a checklist of people, processes, and technology setup with icons for each category.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ssdf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Protect the Software Practice",
          "claim": "Protect the Software practice prevents unauthorized access to code and development artifacts, ensuring confidentiality during the development lifecycle.",
          "example": "Source code repositories use role-based access control to restrict who can view or modify specific modules.",
          "visualDirection": "Draw a locked vault icon over a code repository with access control badges indicating restricted permissions.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ssdf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Produce Well-Secured Software Practice",
          "claim": "Produce Well-Secured Software addresses security requirements, design, verification, and release, providing evidence that selected practices were performed.",
          "example": "Developers complete a security requirements document and run automated verification tests before deploying the software.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a pipeline diagram showing requirements, design, verification, and release stages with checkmarks.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ssdf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Respond to Vulnerabilities Practice",
          "claim": "Respond to Vulnerabilities practice identifies, analyzes, remediates, and learns from discovered weaknesses, closing the security feedback loop.",
          "example": "A security audit finds a buffer overflow, which is patched, and the team updates their threat model based on the lesson learned.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a circular feedback loop with arrows labeled identify, analyze, remediate, and learn.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ssdf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Security Reasoning Process",
          "claim": "Security reasoning identifies assets, trust boundaries, threats, controls, evidence, residual risk, and responsibilities to guide evaluation decisions.",
          "example": "An analyst maps a web application's database as an asset, defines the browser as a trust boundary, and lists SQL injection as a threat.",
          "visualDirection": "Create a flowchart connecting assets to trust boundaries, then to threats, controls, and residual risk calculations.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ssdf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Cryptographic Standards and Algorithms",
          "claim": "Cryptographic standards define approved algorithms and parameters, ensuring that encryption supports confidentiality when keys and modes are used correctly.",
          "example": "AES-256 is an approved algorithm that provides confidentiality for data at rest when implemented with proper key management.",
          "visualDirection": "List approved algorithms like AES and RSA with their corresponding security parameters and use cases.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-cryptographic-standards"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Modes of Operation and Key Management",
          "claim": "Modes of operation define how a primitive processes data larger than one block, while key management covers generation, distribution, storage, use, rotation, revocation, and  ",
          "example": "CBC mode processes multi-block data by chaining ciphertext blocks, and key management ensures keys are rotated regularly to limit exposure.",
          "visualDirection": "Illustrate a block cipher processing multiple data blocks sequentially, alongside a key lifecycle diagram showing rotation and revocation steps.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-cryptographic-standards"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Security Evaluation and Practical Application",
          "claim": "Security depends on the complete protocol and implementation, not only an algorithm name, and validation checks implementations against specified requirements.",
          "example": "A system using a standard encryption algorithm fails security evaluation because the implementation has a side-channel vulnerability.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a comparison chart between algorithm name vs. implementation security, highlighting side-channel risks and validation checkpoints.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-cryptographic-standards"]
        }
      ]}
